<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kapara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 10th–9th century BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kapara was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-halaf">Guzana</a>, the Aramaean kingdom of Bit-Bahiani at Tell Halaf in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, probably in the 10th or 9th century BCE. He built a palace whose entrance had colossal statues of deities standing on animals, and whose walls were lined with dozens of carved stone reliefs, excavated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-von-oppenheim">Max von Oppenheim</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kapara.jpg" alt="Kapara" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Max von Oppenheim · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Max_von_Oppenheim._Tell_Halaf._1912.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
